\begin{description}
\item[* name:] tobjTOobjcontrol
\item[* relevant examples:] froto el suelo hasta dejarlo limpio 
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) Spanish
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] xpproptoclause
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class) tc_fincontrol
\item[* date of creation:] 22-MAR-1991 14:28:20  
\item[* author:] Elena Pinillos
\item[* copied from:]
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] identifies the object of the subordinate clause with the object 
of the main clause and substitutes the first one bij the apropriate clitic, if 
the main verb is nonreflexive
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals)
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:] 
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:] subrules with CNVAR 
in object position dissapeared because in those constructions, the variable is 
always within an NPVAR; the spliting into subrules was necessary in order to 
substitute the NPVAR bij the apropiate clitic
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:]
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:]
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item dictionary:\\ 

\item rules:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[\ \ consequences in the grammar:]
\item[\ \ translation aspects:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate} 
\item within this language:\\

\item in other languages:\\

\end{enumerate}
